    "summary": "Frost '82: high‑energy barn burner with fiery Brent and scorching Crazy Fingers",
    "review": "October 10, 1982 at Frost Amphitheatre is one of those classic early‑’80s “barn burners” that still makes you grin when the tape spins. The band rides a relentless high‑energy current from the opening New Minglewood Blues straight into a soaring Sugaree and rolls through a tight first set that builds momentum with China Cat > Rider, then explodes on the second set. Highlights include a monster Playing In The Band → Crazy Fingers → Lost Sailor → Saint of Circumstance sequence, a searing China Cat/​Rider jam, and an uplifting double encore of Satisfaction and It’s All Over Now Baby Blue. Brent Mydland is absolutely on fire – his organ solo on Minglewood and the gritty, gospel‑tinged comping throughout lift every jam, while Jerry Garcia rides high on a “machine‑gun” tone, shredding leads and delivering soaring solos that feel fresh even after three decades. Phil’s bass punches through the mix, especially on Lost Sailor → Saint and the China/Rider segment, anchoring the improvisations. Bob Weir adds his trademark lyrical flourishes and a memorable spoken‑word rain on Looks Like Rain, keeping the crowd engaged. The dual drummers drive the show with tight, propulsive grooves that push the band to its peak before the final Satisfaction romp. All told, this night captures the Dead at a point where the new keyboard fire meets the classic improvisational chemistry – a must‑listen for any Deadhead.",
